  "subtitle": "The VHS console that painted 8-bit sprites over live video — interactive television, 1988-style",
  "why_scout_cares": "The View-Master Interactive Vision (1988/1989) is the third corner of the museum's VHS-interaction triptych. Where the Bandai Terebikko used audio tones in the VHS tape as a data channel and the Action Max used VHS brightness flashes as a target stream, the Interactive Vision generated its own 8-bit sprite overlays that composited over the live VHS video in real time, with dual audio tracks for branching narratives. The console itself — not the tape — produced the interactive graphics. The controller was a standard joystick + 5 buttons, but the system's real interface was the composite video signal itself: the console mixed its own sprite graphics with the VHS output, creating a hybrid visual layer. It was a dedicated interactive-movie appliance that anticipated the CD-i and Sega CD by 3-4 years. Seven titles were released, including Sesame Street, Muppets, and Disney games. The dual-audio-track technique (two different reactions from the same character, recorded to fit mouth movements) was a clever low-bandwidth solution to interactive storytelling. The system completes the museum's collection of VHS-based interaction paradigms.",
  "search_terms": ["View-Master Interactive Vision", "View-Master Ideal Group", "ACTV", "interactive VHS console", "dual audio track VHS", "sprite overlay video game", "interactive movie 1988"],
